WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:24.340
Everybody.

9
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.320
Hello.

10
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.640
Are you ready for Season 7, Episode 20, Training Day?

11
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.500
I am so ready.

12
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.340
I laughed so flippin' hard watching this episode.

13
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:43.120
I feel like we need to give people an update on our studio situation since we did talk about it last week, Angela.

14
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:43.940
Okay.

15
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.780
I think they have the lease on these offices for, what, maybe three more days?

16
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.040
Maybe three more hours.

17
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.280
We've come in to the Earwolf offices.

18
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.280
There is literally nothing here.

19
00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.220
It feels like three more hours.

20
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.260
Like, it really does.

21
00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.660
Because there's nothing.

22
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.780
I thought, oh, when I get there, maybe I'll get a glass of water.

23
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.000
There's nothing.

24
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.060
There's no glasses.

25
00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:12.040
Like Dunder Mifflin going through a transition, we are also going through a transition of space.

26
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.300
They're going through a transition of manager.

27
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.700
Look at you, girl.

28
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:16.660
Look at you.

29
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.980
We're going through a transition of space.

30
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.180
Well, listen, Training Day was written by Daniel Chun.

31
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.100
It was directed by Paul Lieberstein.

32
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:26.340
And here is your summary.

33
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.140
The Dunder Mifflin-Scranton branch has found a replacement manager.

34
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.620
His name is D'Angelo Vickers.

35
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.480
But Michael is having a hard time letting go, especially because the employees seem to be fawning over D'Angelo.

36
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.860
Meanwhile, Dwight is struggling with the fact that he was not even considered for the manager position.

37
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.780
Oh, that is a blow.

38
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:49.760
Mm-hmm.

39
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.340
Fast fact number one.

40
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.020
I mean, D'Angelo Vickers was played by Will Ferrell.

41
00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.460
We were all so giddy.

42
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:00.260
Remember, Jenna?

43
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:05.060
We just could not wait to show up on set the first day and be in scenes with him.

44
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:05.860
Yes.

45
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.540
This began our four-episode arc with Will.

46
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.240
We had a fan question from Sierra P.

47
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.120
in Oregon.

48
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.820
How did Will Ferrell end up with this role?

49
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.940
Was it specifically written for him?

50
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:19.720
Yes, Sierra.

51
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.740
This role was written for Will.

52
00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.980
And here is the crazy way that he ended up on our show.

53
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:33.580
Will called the producers at the beginning of the season and offered to come in at some point to do a cameo because he knew it was Steve's last season.

54
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.100
And our producers were like, hey, how about a show?

55
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:39.900
Like, what, what, what?

56
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:40.660
Yeah, okay.

57
00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:41.300
Yeah.

58
00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:41.780
They were so happy.

59
00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.460
Where you show up for four episodes.

60
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.040
And he totally agreed.

61
00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.760
When we announced Will's arc to the press, we made our press release.

62
00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.440
Paul Lieberstein, who was the showrunner at the time, had this very funny quote.

63
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.920
He said, quote,

64
00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.620
We found Steve Carell when he was nothing but a movie star, and we turned him into a television star.

65
00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:10.220
We are proud to continue the office's tradition of discovering famous talent, and we hope that one day America gets a good look at Will Ferrell.

66
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.880
They'll see what we see.

67
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.200
Tremendous raw sexuality.

68
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.700
That was his quote to welcome Will Ferrell to the show.

69
00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.160
All right, fast fact number two is a fan question from Bernice C.

70
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.700
in Upland, California, who asks,

71
00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:32.600
Was it ever a possibility that Will Ferrell was going to be the permanent Scranton-Dunder Mifflin manager?

72
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.620
No, it was not a possibility.

73
00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.140
And we all knew that.

74
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.720
We were just lucky to get him.

75
00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:40.960
Yeah, totally.

76
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.700
We actually asked Greg Daniels about Will joining the show, and here's what he had to say.

77
00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.600
I think there was a feeling of, can the show survive without Steve?

78
00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.200
And everybody wanted it to.

79
00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:03.000
We all felt like there was so much vitality in the ensemble caSt.

80
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.880
There were more stories to tell.

81
00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.480
Steve did not want to kill the show with his exit.

82
00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:16.620
And so there was this feeling of, don't save his exit to the very, very end of the season,

83
00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.340
and then have all summer for fans to be like, well, I'm done.

84
00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.660
You know, we wanted to have him exit a little before the end of the season

85
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:29.440
and then have some sort of treat for people to keep watching.

86
00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.600
And so Will Ferrell was the treat.

87
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:37.460
We were all very excited to have Will Ferrell, and he was good friends with Steve.

88
00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:45.600
And it seemed like, you know, something that signaled to people, like, life would go on.

89
00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:51.660
There would be exciting, you know, other comedy people that we could bring in.

90
00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.180
And it also took a little pressure off, probably.

91
00:04:54.340 --> 00:05:01.020
Like, whoever would have been the next boss right after Steve, it would have been very difficult, probably.

92
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.220
It was good to have a little palate cleanser in there.

93
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.340
So Will was our treat and our palate cleanser.

94
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.100
Mm-hmm.

95
00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:19.960
Writer Danny Chun said that it was a huge relief because everyone was so nervous about what is that first episode going to be without Steve.

96
00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.940
And knowing that they would have Will for that episode as a bridge was such a gift for the writers.

97
00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:26.400
I bet.

98
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.620
Like, no pressure.

99
00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.120
And at this time, too, they had not picked a new manager.

100
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.960
I remember the hubbub behind the scenes.

101
00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.380
So much hubbub.

102
00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.920
They had so many people coming in and out, and there were names being thrown around.

103
00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.100
And you'd see people coming in for meetings.

104
00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:45.840
And it would be like, psst, psst, psst, psst, psSt.

105
00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:46.520
Who's that?

106
00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.040
Who's that?

107
00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:47.420
Who is it?

108
00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:47.760
What?

109
00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:48.500
Oh, my God.

110
00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:50.460
That's what we would do, guys.

111
00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:51.160
It is.

112
00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.440
We wouldn't even say words.

113
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:53.940
No, we would just say, psst, psst, psst, psSt.

114
00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:54.480
Oh, my gosh.

115
00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:54.800
I'm so sorry.

116
00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:55.240
Yeah.

117
00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:02.380
Isn't that what you do on stage if you have to do, like, background party atmosphere?

118
00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.100
Do you remember that in high school?

119
00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.880
We'd be like, spaghetti, spaghetti.

120
00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:11.600
Go to get a ball of spaghetti, psst, psst, psst, psst, psst, psst, psst, psst, psSt.

121
00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:13.280
All right.

122
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.540
Fastback number three.

123
00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.420
This is a fan question from Becca H.

124
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:17.620
in Glasgow, Scotland.

125
00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:20.180
What was it like to work with Will Ferrell?

126
00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.840
Was this the first time most of you were meeting him?

127
00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:24.820
Ann, do you want to go first?

128
00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:26.040
Sure.

129
00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.440
I had never met Will.

130
00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:28.780
I was so excited.

131
00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:30.160
I was such a big fan.

132
00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:36.220
And he was the most kind, just normal fella.

133
00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.800
Just felt like you were talking to your neighbor across, like, the hedge in your front lawn.

134
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.640
He was just wonderful.

135
00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:47.880
I just remember, I have such a distinct memory, walking into the soundstage one afternoon,

136
00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.980
and he was just right outside.

137
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.640
He had just been talking to his kids.

138
00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:58.940
And I walked up, and we talked about being parents, and he was just lovely.

139
00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:01.680
I just absolutely liked him so much as a person.

140
00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.980
And then, of course, I could not stop laughing in every scene with him.

141
00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.400
He was so funny.

142
00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:09.420
He is so naturally funny, too.

143
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.760
He'll say a sentence that is, there's nothing about the sentence that's funny, but when he

144
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:14.740
says it, it's funny.

145
00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.420
Or just a look.

146
00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:16.980
Mm-hmm.

147
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.480
Just his expressions would just kill me.

148
00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:26.380
Well, I had had the chance to work with Will before on Blades of Glory, so I knew Will.

149
00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.960
But I think I've told this story before.

150
00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:35.660
For whatever reason, every time I meet Will again or I encounter Will after all these

151
00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:41.220
years, and it happened here as well, I always introduce myself to him with my first and last

152
00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.140
name and remind him how he knows me.

153
00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:47.520
And he's like, Jenna, I know who you are.

154
00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:49.960
We've worked together multiple times.

155
00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:51.140
Stop introducing yourself.

156
00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.020
I'm like, hey, Will, Jenna Fisher.

157
00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.180
We worked together on Blades of Glory and then again on The Office.

158
00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.840
And he's like, Jenna, I know you.

159
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:00.960
But I don't know.

160
00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:04.480
He just, he's such a down-to-earth guy.

161
00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:11.220
But his stature and his, like, just legendary status, it always makes me feel like, how many

162
00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.120
people does this guy encounter in a day?

163
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.420
And also, he talks to every single person.

164
00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:16.660
He's so friendly.

165
00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:17.680
He's like you, Angela.

166
00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.580
Oh, well, thank you.

167
00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:26.300
I mean, 100% Will Ferrell is becoming best friends with whoever's sitting next to him on

168
00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:26.680
a plane.

169
00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:28.020
Oh, yeah.

170
00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.220
You have that in common.

171
00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:30.600
This is true.

172
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:33.800
Well, that's all I got.

173
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:35.960
All my fast facts were about Will Ferrell.

174
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:37.700
I mean, this is his first episode.

175
00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:38.580
What can I say?

176
00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:42.580
I have a fun DVD tidbit for those of you that have the DVDs.

177
00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:46.780
There is an awesome extended producer's cut of this episode.

178
00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:51.340
If you love Will Ferrell and you love Will and Steve together, you're going to want to

179
00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:56.320
go watch the extended cut because there is so much more of D'Angelo and Michael.

180
00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.500
And I'm going to sprinkle a few scenes in throughout the episode that were just

181
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:01.020
too good.

182
00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:01.740
I have to share.

183
00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.420
I could watch hours of those two.

184
00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.080
So this is very good news to me.

185
00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:07.320
Well, what do you think?

186
00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:08.100
Should we take a break?

187
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.040
And then when we come back, we break down this episode?

188
00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:13.860
I think so, because two strangers are going to find a friendship at a bar.

189
00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:15.080
Mm-hmm.

190
00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:16.080
Mm-hmm.

191
00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:25.620
We are back.

192
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.360
And can you guess what this sound is?

193
00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:31.110
That's right.

194
00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:33.310
That's a Kahlua sombrero.

195
00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:34.410
Michael's drink.

196
00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.130
You know we had to try it.

197
00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:37.930
Sam, Cassie, Angela.

198
00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:39.850
How do we like Michael's drink?

199
00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:42.170
It is refreshing.

200
00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.010
Oh, that's wonderful.

201
00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:45.690
I know.

202
00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:46.170
I know.

203
00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:47.150
I'm here for it.

204
00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:48.410
Guys, what do you think?

205
00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:50.170
Ooh, it's really nice.

206
00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:51.370
It's really nice.

207
00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.150
I was expecting a cringe, and there's no cringe.

208
00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:59.090
According to the Kahlua website, it is made with two ounces of Kahlua to three ounces of

209
00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:03.690
milk, and it has approximately 518 calories per glass.

210
00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:04.610
Oh, my gosh.

211
00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:11.050
I mean, it seems like a shocking amount of calories for this refreshing drink.

212
00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:16.410
They also, incidentally, on the Kahlua website, I noticed they had a drink called the Orange

213
00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:23.230
Blonde, and it really reminded me of Jim and Pam's drink of Baileys and orange juice,

214
00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:30.150
because the photo on the website, it's not selling the Orange Blonde very well.

215
00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:35.990
It looks like that curdled drink that we drank before, but I don't know.

216
00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:38.110
I didn't bring the ingredients for that one.

217
00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:40.110
I don't even know why I'm bringing it up.

218
00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.030
I'm okay to pass on the Orange Blonde.

219
00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:47.150
When you said Orange Blonde, I just thought of, like, those summers when I was lifeguarding

220
00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:50.950
as a teenager, and we put a whole bunch of sun in our hair, and we all, like, were these

221
00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:52.850
orange blondes.

222
00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:57.850
Well, Jenna and Sam made this Kahlua Sombrero, and it is so good.

223
00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:58.830
I Googled it.

224
00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.270
I didn't even know if it was a real drink, and I looked it up.

225
00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.710
Here's one thing I found that I thought was interesting.

226
00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:07.330
A lot of these types of drinks became popular during Prohibition.

227
00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:07.990
Oh.

228
00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:11.050
According to the internet, cocktail fans had to get creative.

229
00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:16.350
In this case, people that liked brandy or whiskey, they could camouflage their drinks

230
00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:19.570
with milk, and it was harder to detect the alcohol.

231
00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:20.710
Yeah.

232
00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:23.010
Lady, can I make an observation?

233
00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:23.770
What?

234
00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:26.890
When you do your deep dives, it just tickles me each time.

235
00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:28.670
It's one of my favorite things you do.

236
00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:29.210
What?

237
00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:30.570
Did I say according to the internet?

238
00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:30.950
Yes.

239
00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:32.710
You credit the entire internet.

240
00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.770
Not a specific website.

241
00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:39.490
Just the whole internet says this thing, and it just every time.

242
00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:40.730
I love it.

243
00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:46.050
Well, I usually look at a few different sources, so I just credit the whole shebang.

244
00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:52.550
Well, Michael is going to meet a stranger who's also having a drink at the bar.

245
00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:56.070
They're going to strike up a little conversation and become fast friends.

246
00:11:56.530 --> 00:12:01.710
I thought this toast was one of the best toasts I'd ever heard, and since we're drinking a

247
00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:06.250
Kahlua sombrero together, we've got to hear Michael and D'Angelo's toaSt.

248
00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.630
Leaving my company after 19 years.

249
00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:11.310
I'll drink to that.

