We just got to bring her in.

1094
00:49:27.460 --> 00:49:28.780
He needs to see her in person.

1095
00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:29.220
Yep.

1096
00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:29.680
Mm-hmm.

1097
00:49:29.680 --> 00:49:35.580
Well, also during this scene, D'Angelo is going to ask people, how can he be the best

1098
00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:36.600
manager for them?

1099
00:49:36.720 --> 00:49:39.420
Like, how could he make things even better?

1100
00:49:39.740 --> 00:49:41.880
And Michael says, listen, be honeSt.

1101
00:49:41.920 --> 00:49:42.900
It's fine.

1102
00:49:43.540 --> 00:49:47.940
So Oscar suggests maybe we could have some improved communication.

1103
00:49:48.460 --> 00:49:53.620
You know, currently, every single memo is marked urgent, and it's very confusing.

1104
00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:59.060
And then Michael says he marks them urgent, because if he didn't, then no one would read

1105
00:49:59.060 --> 00:50:01.220
them, but that he has a special system.

1106
00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:04.160
Urgent A, urgent B, urgent C, urgent D.

1107
00:50:04.400 --> 00:50:05.600
Everyone knows urgent D.

1108
00:50:05.720 --> 00:50:08.120
You can just ignore urgent A is really urgent.

1109
00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:12.000
Lady, this reminded me.

1110
00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:19.260
Have I ever told you about the guy I worked for who had the color-coded system for his

1111
00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:20.700
mail and other memos?

1112
00:50:20.700 --> 00:50:24.660
We've been doing the podcast so long, I can't even remember if I've told this story before.

1113
00:50:24.800 --> 00:50:28.340
Wait, is this the guy that made you FedEx from the airport?

1114
00:50:28.580 --> 00:50:29.400
Is this the same guy?

1115
00:50:29.420 --> 00:50:29.660
Yes, same guy.

1116
00:50:29.900 --> 00:50:30.420
Oh, my God.

1117
00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:32.040
I don't think I know the folder story.

